<beans xmlns="http://www.springframework.org/schema/beans"
       xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.springframework.org/schema/beans
                           http://www.springframework.org/schema/beans/spring-beans.xsd">

    <!--
      Patient portal integration (issue #3475).

      This file is NOT picked up by the classpath:applicationContext*.xml pattern in web.xml.
      OscarSpringContextLoader overrides createWebApplicationContext and never reads
      contextConfigLocation: it builds its own list from applicationContext.xml plus one file per
      ModuleNames entry. It is imported explicitly from applicationContext.xml, and deleting that
      import silently unwires the portal rather than failing.

      EVERY BEAN HERE IS lazy-init. This is the load-bearing detail, not a tuning choice.

      PatientPortalSettings.fromCarlosProperties() throws when the portal is unconfigured, which is
      correct at a call site — a portal call must never silently become a no-op a clinic would
      misread as "the invitation was sent". But an eager bean whose factory throws fails the whole
      Spring context refresh, so that same fail-closed rule would stop CARLOS starting at all for
      every clinic that does not use the portal. Most will not. Lazy initialisation keeps the
      failure where it belongs: at the moment someone tries to use a portal that is not there.

      EVERY BEAN IS ALSO autowire-candidate="false", as defence in depth: an injection point
      elsewhere that matches one of these types by type would resolve it during refresh and so
      defeat lazy-init. There is no such injection point today, and this attribute is cheap
      insurance against one being added.

      Be careful with the history here. A deployed CARLOS did fail to start with these beans
      present, and the fix commit blamed the byType beans in spring_ws.xml. That explanation does
      not hold up: those two beans are the only autowire="byType" declarations in the file, XML
      byType autowiring is setter-based, and neither class has a writable property of any type in
      this package. The commit bundled three changes — the missing import above, a constructor-arg
      naming a bean that does not exist (SecurityInfoManager is an interface; the @Service
      implementation registers as securityInfoManagerImpl), and this attribute — and the trigger
      was never isolated to one of them. The first two are independently proven defects. Treat
      this attribute as unproven-but-harmless, not as a fix with a known mechanism.

      Lookup by type through SpringUtils still works: autowire-candidate governs autowiring, not
      getBean.

      PatientPortalService holds a pooled HTTP client, hence destroy-method="close".
    -->

    <bean id="patientPortalSettings"
          class="io.github.carlos_emr.carlos.integration.patientportal.PatientPortalSettings"
          factory-method="fromCarlosProperties"
          lazy-init="true"
          autowire-candidate="false"/>

    <bean id="patientPortalService"
          class="io.github.carlos_emr.carlos.integration.patientportal.PatientPortalService"
          destroy-method="close"
          lazy-init="true"
          autowire-candidate="false">
        <constructor-arg ref="patientPortalSettings"/>
    </bean>

    <!--
      The resolver reads SecurityInfoManager only, so it constructs whether or not a portal is
      configured. Still lazy for symmetry: nothing should touch this package on a deployment that
      does not use it.
    -->
    <bean id="portalStaffContextResolver"
          class="io.github.carlos_emr.carlos.integration.patientportal.PortalStaffContextResolver"
          lazy-init="true"
          autowire-candidate="false">
        <!-- SecurityInfoManager is an interface; the @Service implementation registers under the
             default name derived from its class, so the reference is to the implementation. -->
        <constructor-arg ref="securityInfoManagerImpl"/>
    </bean>

    <!--
      Invitation delivery (issue #3854). An unset public URL or sender only makes the workflow refuse
      to send, so an unconfigured invitation workflow cannot stop the rest of the portal integration.
      A configured but invalid public URL does throw, like every other portal setting, so callers that
      have something else to show must resolve this bean where they can answer for it: the panel
      resolves it inside the try that guards its deliveries section. The service is created only after
      PatientPortalSettings.isConfigured(), like every other portal action, because it holds the
      portal client.

      The component-scanned collaborators register under names derived from their classes; the
      wiring test pins those names.
    -->
    <bean id="portalInviteSettings"
          class="io.github.carlos_emr.carlos.integration.patientportal.PortalInviteSettings"
          factory-method="fromCarlosProperties"
          lazy-init="true"
          autowire-candidate="false"/>

    <bean id="portalInviteDeliveryService"
          class="io.github.carlos_emr.carlos.integration.patientportal.PortalInviteDeliveryService"
          lazy-init="true"
          autowire-candidate="false">
        <constructor-arg index="0" ref="patientPortalService"/>
        <constructor-arg index="1" ref="patientPortalSettings"/>
        <constructor-arg index="2" ref="portalInviteSettings"/>
        <constructor-arg index="3" ref="emailManager"/>
        <constructor-arg index="4" ref="patientPortalInviteDeliveryDaoImpl"/>
        <constructor-arg index="5" ref="emailConfigDaoImpl"/>
        <constructor-arg index="6" ref="emailLogDaoImpl"/>
    </bean>

</beans>
